  "scope": "passage",
  "structured": true,
  "speaker": "Abhinavagupta (commentator voice)",
  "question": "The tṛtīyo vimarśa established the pramāṇa, its fruit, and the primacy of the\nknower. Now, in closing, Abhinavagupta asks the practical question: given all this, **how does one\nactually attain the Lord** (parameśvaratā)? The answer runs through the fourfold \"work\" (caturvidha\nkṛtya), the Lord's grace (prasāda), initiation (dīkṣā), the vision-yoga (dhyāna), knowledge (jñāna),\nand devotion (bhāvanā) — and ends in the *jīvanmukta*, the one liberated while living, who needs no\nfurther rebirth.",
  "premises": [
   "A supreme (pārameśvara) work must be accomplished [by the aspirant]; even if impossible in itself, it\nis assumed as possible. The \"effort\" (prayatna) is the life of the fourfold work (caturvidha kṛtya).\n**And only by the Lord's grace (tatprasādād eva)** — when the Lord's self is pleased, with the\nabandoning of the turbidity of body-and-the-rest conceit — is one able to cross the great ocean of\ndelusion. In the Lord-taught śāstra, established by bhāvanā [devotion], the saṃskāra, the\ndīkṣādikriyā [initiatory rite], the dhyānayoga [vision-yoga], the yoga, the jñāna [knowledge] — by\ntheir all-around capacity — [the aspirant] abandons, leaves, the heya-form [the to-be-abandoned]\nworld — the knower-made world of body, breath, the eight-fold city, the void — the world whose mark is\nthe support/object of enjoyment — the world of the lokas and the beings — and, by the gate of the\nLord's grace and the guru's grace, in the dīkṣā and the rest, being graced by the param-Lord, he\nbrings the lordship (aiśvarya) to manifestation in himself. This is the connection.",
   "Here the capacity of all the dīkṣā-and-the-rest is stated: even in this birth, the attainment of the\nparam-Lord — although vision and knowledge are primary, the knowledge is preceded by the dīkṣā. (Cites\nmā. vi. 4.6: \"There is no eligibility for the yoga of Śaṅkara without the dīkṣā; and it is declared\ntwofold, by the distinction of kriyā and jñāna.\")",
   "But if one, without practicing the dhyānayoga, is assured by the jñāna-dīkṣā [knowledge-initiation]\nalone — then what? Answer: the **bhāvanā** (devotion/contemplation). Some (the yogins) attain a *share\nof the vibhūti* — a portion of all-knowing-ness and all-doing-ness, the capacity of the\nhigher-knowledge-instrument, marked by the eight-fold vibhūti (aṇimā and the rest), pertaining to this\nand that loka. But even with the manifest existence of the higher [vibhūti], there is no loss of the\npresent [goal] — and even by reason this is established (\"nahi\").",
   "The ātma-being and the param-ātman are both present together without conflict (paraspara-sannidhāna).\nNo conflict from the prāṇa's ātma-being present with the param-ātman; but a conflict of the\nself-experiencing self-shining-ness? — No: if the param-ātman is present, then he is of the nature of\nthe self-shining one. But then how is the prāṇa's ātma-being? — By the param-ātman's own free\nmanifestation (svātantryeṇa prathā), whose power it is; the prāṇa, etc., shine as ātma by that\nprabhāva. The prāṇa and the rest, too, have the touched-by-vedakatā (knower-ness), even in\nvedyatā (known-ness) — the \"one\"-grasping. And even non-beings (avastu) are thus; even the non-being\nis [shown]. The object (prameya) is remembered by the power of the break of experience; but the\nknower-form, whose essence is the un-broken light, is not remembered (spa. kā. 13: \"not thus is\nremembered-ness the essence of this-and-this\"). The established [point]: the Lord-ness is not\nanything new to be proved in the consciousness-essence; the negation of ātma-ness in the prāṇa and the\nrest is not new either (prāṇādīnām iti). The cidrūpa [consciousness-form] — this was established at\nlength (1.3.6, the \"evam anyonyam\" ṭīkā).",
   "In this much, in every way, there is the utter extinction of the delusion-impression (mohakalā-\nsaṃskāra). And on its extinction, by the dissolving of the body-and-the-rest-I-conceit's restraint,\n**there is the param-Lord-ness itself** (parameśvarataiva). But by the Lord's will alone, for some,\neven with hundreds of efforts, the recognition (pratyabhijñāna) does not arise; yet even for him, from\nthe mere entry into the hearing [of the teaching], by the order of the ripening of the impression,\nthere is necessarily the attainment of the own-form. (Cites the āgama: \"Unwavering in self-experience,\nhe is called the jīvanmukta; but he who still constructs the essence — at the fall of the body, he\ngoes to Śiva.\") Abhinava explains: \"like the root-leaves of the old tree of saṃsāra, the sparks of\nthe param-real fire — if the inner-intent is ended, the pure-knowledge-fire blazes.\" Hence, by this,\n**nothing new is established in the self** (na apūrvaṃ kiñcit prasādhya te svātmani) — this is what was\ndeclared at the beginning of the śāstra (1.1.2: \"in the agent, the knower, the own self, the\nādi-siddha\"). And the colophon: the pramāṇatatphalaprameyaparamārthavimarśaḥ tṛtīyaḥ || 3 || — the\nclose of the tṛtīyo vimarśa."
  ],
  "inference": "Decision for L2\nProceed. The argument is understood: the attainment of the Lord is by grace, through the fourfold work\n— dīkṣā (initiation) first, then vision, knowledge, devotion; the prāṇa's ātma-being is the\nparam-ātman's free manifestation; the knower is un-broken and un-remembered (spa. kā. 13); and on the\nextinction of the delusion-impression, the param-Lord-ness itself arises — the jīvanmukta, for whom\nnothing new is established, since the Lord was already the own self.",
  "uncertainties": [
   "The exact working of the \"caturvidha kṛtya\" (the fourfold work) — its items are given via the",
   "The mā. vi. 4.6 citation (eligibility requires dīkṣā) — cited, not re-derived.",
   "The \"mohakalāsaṃskāra\" (delusion-fragment-impression) — the exact ontology of the residual"
  ],
